            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Hindi for a gun, then the Hindi for to tie up."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ठगना",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ठगना — ṭhagnā — \"to cheat; to swindle\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "अगर कोई तुम्हें ठग रहा है, तो पुलिस को बताओ। agar koī tumhẽ ṭhag rahā hai, to pulis ko batāo. If someone is cheating you, tell the police."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Past, with ने (he or she …): उसने मुझे ठगा, usne mujhe ṭhagā."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ठगना (ṭhagnā) is inherited from ṭhagg, a form CDIAL reconstructs (CDIAL 5489), which also lists the noun ṭhag, a swindler. English thug was taken from that noun."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling what happened."
            }
          ]
        },
